/-!
# Tag 0B4Y: the powers of the ideal sheaf of the special fibre

In Situation [Stacks, Tag 0B4W] — a two-dimensional local normal Nagata domain `A` defining a
rational singularity, `S = Spec(A)` with closed point `s`, and a modification `f : X ⟶ S` with
`X` normal — write `𝓘` for the ideal sheaf of the special fibre `E = X_s`. [Stacks, Tag 0B4Y]
asserts that `H⁰(X, 𝓘ⁿ) = 𝔪ⁿ` and `H¹(X, 𝓘ⁿ) = 0`.

The vanishing `H¹(X, 𝓘ⁿ) = 0` follows the source's own route: `𝓘ⁿ = 𝔪ⁿ𝒪_X` is generated by
global sections, namely by the `n`-fold products of a set of generators of `𝔪`, and a globally
generated quasi-coherent module on `X` has vanishing first cohomology by clause (2) of
[Stacks, Tag 0B4X]. This file supplies the generation of the powers; the conclusion itself is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RationalSingularitySituation.isZero_firstCohomology_exceptionalIdealPow_unconditional`
of `ExceptionalIdealPowersExtra.lean`.

The other conclusion, `H⁰(X, 𝓘ⁿ) = 𝔪ⁿ`, again follows the source's own route, under the
source's own additional hypothesis for it — that `E = X_s` is an effective Cartier
divisor. Picking generators of `𝔪` presents `𝓘` as a quotient of `⨁ 𝒪_X` with kernel `ℱ`; the
module `ℱ ⊗ 𝓘ⁿ` is generated by global sections by [Stacks, Tag 0B3B] together with the
reassociation `ℱ ⊗ 𝓘ⁿ ≅ (ℱ ⊗ 𝓘) ⊗ 𝓘ⁿ⁻¹`, so it has vanishing first cohomology by clause (2) of
[Stacks, Tag 0B4X]; the long exact sequence of
`0 ⟶ ℱ ⊗ 𝓘ⁿ ⟶ (𝓘ⁿ)^{⊕μ+1} ⟶ 𝓘ⁿ⁺¹ ⟶ 0` then makes every global section of `𝓘ⁿ⁺¹` a combination
of the generators with coefficients in `H⁰(X, 𝓘ⁿ)`, and the induction runs. This file supplies
the long-exact-sequence step and the case `n = 1`; the conclusion itself is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RationalSingularitySituation.idealSheafGlobalSections_exceptionalIdealPow_unconditional`
of `ExceptionalIdealPowersExtra.lean`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSheafOfModulesPowGeneratingSections`: generating global sections of
  every power of a sheaf of ideals, from generating global sections of the ideal.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSheafGlobalSections`: the global sections of the ideal sheaf of a
  closed subscheme, read as an ideal of the ring of global functions, with
  `AlgebraicGeometry.mem_idealSheafGlobalSections_iff`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSheafGlobalSections_mul_le`,
  `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSheafGlobalSections_pow_zero` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSheafGlobalSections_ker`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSheafGlobalSections_mul`: **the long-exact-sequence step** of the
  source's proof of `H⁰(X, 𝓘ⁿ) = 𝔪ⁿ`, for an arbitrary quasi-compact scheme: if the ideal sheaf
  of `I` is generated by global sections `g`, if `J` is an effective Cartier divisor and if
  `H¹(X, ker(g.π) ⊗ ℐ_J) = 0`, then `Γ(X, ℐ_{I·J}) = Γ(X, ℐ_I) · Γ(X, ℐ_J)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.kernelTensorIdealPowGeneratingSections`: `ℱ ⊗ ℐ_{Iⁿ}` is generated by global
  sections, the source's "hence the same holds for `ℱ ⊗ 𝓘ⁿ`".
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RationalSingularitySituation.isIso_globalFunctions`: `Γ(X, 𝒪_X) = A`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RationalSingularitySituation.idealSheafGlobalSections_exceptionalIdeal`:
  `H⁰(X, 𝓘) = 𝔪`, the case `n = 1` of the global-sections conclusion, which needs no hypothesis
  on the special fibre.

## Implementation notes

The sheaf of ideals of the special fibre is `Scheme.Hom.ker` of the inclusion of the fibre, and
`𝓘ⁿ` is the `n`-th power in the `IdemCommSemiring` structure on `Scheme.IdealSheafData`; the
associated sheaf of `𝒪_X`-modules is `Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafOfModules`. Global generation of
the power is built from global generation of the ideal by induction: the multiplication
`𝓘ⁿ ⊗ 𝓘 ⟶ 𝓘ⁿ⁺¹` is an epimorphism (`Stacks.Divisors.epi_idealTensorMul`) and the tensor product
of two globally generated modules is globally generated
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.tensorSheafGeneratingSections`), which is exactly "the products of the
generators generate".

`H⁰(X, ℐ_I)` is read inside `Γ(X, 𝒪_X)` as `AlgebraicGeometry.idealSheafGlobalSections`, the
kernel of restriction to the closed subscheme; `Stacks.Divisors.idealSheafSection` identifies it
with the global sections of the ideal sheaf.

`Γ(X, 𝒪_X) = A` is [Stacks, Tag 0AY8] at `f`, available as
`Stacks.SteinFactorization.isIso_c_of_isNormal`: the base is a normal integral scheme because `A`
is a normal domain, the source is reduced because it is integral, the unique generic point of `X`
lies over the generic point of `Spec(A)` because `f` is birational, and the generic fibre
contributes no functions beyond the function field because it is the spectrum of that field —
which holds because a proper birational morphism is an isomorphism over a dense open
([Stacks, Tag 0BAJ]) and the generic point lies in every dense open.

Cohomology is the one used by [Stacks, Tag 0B4X] in this development,
`((moduleGlobalSections X).rightDerivedPlus i).obj ℱ`; the long-exact-sequence step is stated
with the `ℕ`-indexed `Functor.rightDerived`, which is the indexing
`Stacks.AmpleCohomologicalCriterion.surjective_evaluation_map_g` uses, the two being compared by
`Stacks.DerivedIndexing.isZero_rightDerivedPlus_obj_iff`.

The two inputs of the source's argument not proved in this file — the hypothesis of
[Stacks, Tag 0B4X] (the residue of [Stacks, Tag 02V7] at fibre dimension one) and the global
generation of `𝓘` itself (the source's `𝓘 = 𝔪𝒪_X`, [Stacks, Tag 01JU]) — are supplied in
`ExceptionalIdealPowersExtra.lean`, where both conclusions of [Stacks, Tag 0B4Y] are stated with
no carried hypothesis.

**The reassociation.** The source's "hence the same holds for `ℱ ⊗ 𝓘ⁿ`" is
`kernelTensorIdealPowGeneratingSections`: the case `n = 1` is [Stacks, Tag 0B3B]
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.kernelTensorGeneratingSections`), and the general case follows by
tensoring it with the globally generated `ℐ_{𝓘ⁿ}`, reassociating
`(ℱ ⊗ 𝓘) ⊗ ℐ_{𝓘ⁿ} ≅ ℱ ⊗ (𝓘 ⊗ ℐ_{𝓘ⁿ})` and identifying `𝓘 ⊗ ℐ_{𝓘ⁿ} ≅ ℐ_{𝓘ⁿ⁺¹}`
(`Stacks.Divisors.idealTensorMulIso`, which is where the effective-Cartier hypothesis enters).
The ideal sheaves and `kernelTensorGeneratingSections` are stated over the Tag 01CB tensor
product `Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorStalkRingedSpace.tensorSheaf`, so the associator used is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.TensorStalkRingedSpace.tensorSheafAssociator`, which is stated over that
same tensor product; the Tag 05NA associator cannot be applied here directly, because the
elaborator cannot afford the nested comparison of the two constructions — see the
implementation notes of
`StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.TensorAssociatorRingedSpace`.

